use Libraries.Web.Page.QuorumPage

/*
    Tutorial: Button.
    Converted from the old Lesson page by plan 13's generator: the same
    words, now without Tailwind.
*/
class UserInterfacesButton is QuorumPage
    // A double quote, for code and words that hold one.
    text dq = ""

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/ui/button.html")
        // The name lists and links use, and the series and track it belongs
        // to: its section and picture.
        SetShortTitle("Button")
        SetSectionName("User Interfaces")
        // For search engines and link previews, and what lists of pages say.
        SetDescription("Learn about Buttons in the Quorum programming language.")
        SetSummary("how to use Buttons")
    end

    action Setup
        dq = dq:GetDoubleQuote()
        SetWebPageTitle("Button Tutorial - Quorum Programming Language")
        Title("Tutorial: Button")
        Paragraph("This tutorial tells you how to use Buttons in Quorum")
        AnIntroductionToButtonsInQuorum()
        StandardButtons()
        AddingBehaviorToAButton()
        RunTheProgram()
    end

    private action AnIntroductionToButtonsInQuorum
        Section("An Introduction to Buttons in Quorum")
        Paragraph("In this tutorial, we will learn how to use Buttons in the Quorum Game Engine. A Button is a simple interface tool which executes a Behavior when clicked.")
        Paragraph("For this tutorial, we will create a Button that says "):DoubleQuote():Text("Hello world!"):DoubleQuote():Text(" aloud and outputs it as text each time it is pressed. To start, create a new Game Application project.")
    end

    private action StandardButtons
        Section("Standard Buttons")
        Paragraph("In order to use a Button, we must include the Button library with the following use statement:")
        CodeExample("use Libraries.Interface.Controls.Button")
        Paragraph("Since we want the Button to be usable as soon as the game begins, we'll create it in the CreateGame action. After we create the Button, we can set its position like we would a Drawable, using the SetPosition action. We can also set a name which will be the text the Button displays on the screen. Then we can add it to the game, giving us the following lines of code:")
        CodeExample("Button button
button:SetName(" + dq + "Click me!" + dq + ")
button:SetPosition(300, 300)
SetFocus(button)
Add(button)")
        Paragraph("We also added a SetFocus call with the Button as a parameter. We do this so that the Button is "):Strong("accessible,"):Text(" which means we can activate it using only the keyboard. When the Button is focused, you can either click it or press the Spacebar to activate.")
        Paragraph("So far, the Main class is as follows:")
        CodeExample("use Libraries.Game.Game
use Libraries.Interface.Controls.Button
use Libraries.Interface.Layouts.ManualLayout

class Main is Game
    action Main
        StartGame()
    end

    action CreateGame
        ManualLayout layout
        SetLayout(layout)
        Button button
        button:SetName(" + dq + "Click me!" + dq + ")
        button:SetPosition(300, 300)
        SetFocus(button)
        Add(button)
    end
end")
        Paragraph("When we run the program, the Button appears in the Game window as shown below:")
        Image("media/userInterface/ButtonSmall.png", "This image shows the Button's appearance in the game window.")
        Paragraph("You might notice that the Button seems small for the screen and might be wondering how we could resize it. Right now the Button is sized according the text it displays, and to resize we need to alter the Button's LayoutProperties. However, this is not necessary for the purposes of this tutorial, so for now we'll simply say that Buttons use a Layout to organize their contents. The specifics on how to resize and alter a Button's Layout will be covered later in the Layout tutorial.")
    end

    private action AddingBehaviorToAButton
        Section("Adding Behavior to a Button")
        Paragraph("Right now, nothing happens when the Button is clicked. To have an action activate when we click the button, we must add a Behavior to the Button.")
        Paragraph("To begin, create a new Quorum Class, which we will name "):DoubleQuote():Text("OutputBehavior.quorum."):DoubleQuote():Text(" This class will use the libraries for Behavior, BehaviorEvent, and Speech. The class itself will inherit the Behavior class, and we will override the Run action to say and output the desired text. This gives the following OutputBehavior class:")
        CodeExample("use Libraries.Interface.Behaviors.Behavior
use Libraries.Interface.Events.BehaviorEvent
use Libraries.Sound.Speech

class OutputBehavior is Behavior

    action Run(BehaviorEvent event)
        Speech speech
        speech:Say(" + dq + "Hello world!" + dq + ", false)
        output " + dq + "Hello world!" + dq + "
    end
end")
        Paragraph("Now that we have our completed OutputBehavior class, we can add it to the Button in our Main class using the Button's SetBehavior action. To do so, return to the CreateGame action, and add the following lines:")
        CodeExample("OutputBehavior behavior
button:SetBehavior(behavior)")
        Paragraph("This complete Main class is as follows:")
        CodeExample("use Libraries.Game.Game
use Libraries.Interface.Controls.Button

class Main is Game

    action Main
        StartGame()
    end

    action CreateGame
        OutputBehavior behavior

        ManualLayout layout
        SetLayout(layout)
        Button button
        button:SetName(" + dq + "Click me!" + dq + ")
        button:SetPosition(300, 300)
        SetFocus(button)
        Add(button)

        button:SetBehavior(behavior)

    end
end")
        Paragraph("Now, when we run the program, clicking the Button or pressing the Spacebar will say and output the "):DoubleQuote():Text("Hello world!"):DoubleQuote():Text(" message.")
        Subsection("Accessibility")
        Paragraph("In our example, we used an action named "):Strong("SetFocus"):Text(", this action allowed for the Button to be used with only the keyboard. The "):Strong("Focus"):Text(" is covered in a later "):Link("tutorial", "/tutorials/ui/focus.html"):Text(", but we use it here to make our User Interface accessible. Accessibility is one of the principles that guides the development of Quorum, and that is why the language Screen Reader is friendly and why Quorum can easily be used to make accessible applications. Focus is what allows the use of a keyboard to interact with the User Interface, and it tells Screen Readers which object's information to read, so use of Focus will be found in most of the User Interface tutorials.")
        Paragraph("Games made in the Quorum Game engine are made to be compatible with a Screen Reader, and when making accessible applications it is important to understand how a Screen Reader will interact with your application. A Screen Reader will read the name and any text that the Focused object displays. In our Button example, we used the "):Strong("SetName"):Text(" action call, which changes both the display text of the Button and what a Screen Reader will read when the Button has Focus. Using "):Strong("SetName"):Text(" and "):Strong("Focus Cycles"):Text(" allows the creation of accessible applications for users with Screen Readers, or for users that cannot use a mouse.")
    end

    private action RunTheProgram
        Section("Run the Program")
        CodeEditorFromProject("Activity: Button", "Button.quorum", "examples/UserInterface/Button/Main.quorum")
    end
end
